It doesn't, not being a living entity. It just has
been programmed to expect that a modestly competent
programmer will write that as something more
self-explanatory, like:

  if
  (
    (
      (bool)
      (
        my_var = foo()
      )
    )
    ==
    true
  )

and so guess that what you wrote is not what you
intended. You are free to choose to code unreadably,
and to ignore the resulting warnings. I wish you the
joy of maintaining your own code if you make that
choice.

Remember, please, that the source code, besides
being grist for the compiler, needs to act as
documentation for the reader / next maintainer, so
that the weird optimizations like omitting the
implied boolean comparision and omitting the cast in
kind of the numeric result to a boolean value should
be left for the compiler to find and do, not be
embedded in visible code to confuse the human
reader.

